# The `collapse-stack` tool (design/stacks.md, "Publishing"): the
# std/git-runner image with this directory's script curried onto it, plus the
# `help` it is described by. It returns VALUES — new commit hashes — and changes
# no tree, so it is not a `@writer`; the agent links the commits it prints.
HELP=<<END
Collapse a stack of source layers into one commit per layer, for publishing each
layer as a branch with a linear history. The layers keep their own merge history;
this only mints new commits beside them.

For each layer in order, C = commit(tree = the layer tip's tree, parent = the
previous C, or `onto` for the first layer, message = that layer's message), with
the author and committer copied from the layer tip. The same input always mints
the same commits. Refused, naming both, unless each layer's tip already contains
the one below it (or `onto`, for the first): merge a changed lower layer up first.

Prints one line per layer, `<layer name> <commit>`. To publish, link each commit
into the conversation (`caos get-hash <commit> /cas/c; ln -s /cas/c <path>`) and
publish_source them bottom to top with rewrite=true.
@param {tree} stack Conversation path of the directory holding the layer gitlinks, e.g. `feature`.
@param {commit} onto Full hash of the commit the first layer is published onto: the base branch's tip that layer 1 last merged.
@param {array} layers One entry per layer, bottom first: `<entry name in stack> <commit message>`, e.g. `01-core Add the parser core`.
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/git-runner --worker1:@=worker.sh --help=$HELP
